# Observability & SRE

## Telemetry
Every HTTP request and asynchronous job carries a correlation/trace ID.
Critical logs are structured and contain:
- timestamp;
- severity;
- service/module;
- event name;
- correlation_id;
- tenant_id only when safe/authorized for operational telemetry;
- resource identifiers;
- outcome/latency.

Never log secrets, raw recordings, transcripts, full payment credentials or sensitive KYC contents.

## Business-impact metrics
Track:
- tenant DB routing success/failure;
- tenant DB connection pool saturation;
- migration backlog/failure;
- recording ingestion backlog/failure/orphans;
- call setup/answer/completion/failure;
- recording artifact readiness;
- provider latency/error;
- API p95/p99;
- queue depth/age/failure;
- payment/webhook lag;
- commission reconciliation mismatch;
- payout aging/failure;
- KYC queue aging;
- webhook retry rate;
- tenant isolation/security violations.

## Alert priority
P0/P1:
- suspected tenant isolation breach;
- wrong-tenant DB routing;
- financial inconsistency;
- call routing outage at scale;
- recording exposure/access-control failure;
- widespread payment/webhook failure.

## Incident response
Contain -> preserve evidence -> identify blast radius -> mitigate -> verify -> communicate -> RCA -> corrective action.
Never destroy evidence to make dashboards green.

## Capacity
For every scale change estimate:
concurrent calls, DB connections per tenant, active tenant count, queue throughput, recording ingest bandwidth, storage growth, webhook rate, provider limits and hotspot tenants.

Avoid multiplying expensive connections by total tenant count. Use bounded pools and active-tenant scheduling.
